--TEST--
`rule:types/decimal` and `rule:types/arithmetic`: both named divisions refuse a zero divisor, and `divRound` refuses a scale past the type's own bound
--FILE--
<?nvs
decimal $one = 1;
decimal $none = 0;

// § 3's "`/ 0` throws `ArithmeticError`", at both members that divide -- each
// saying its own name, because the two are separate call sites to fix.
try {
    echo Core\Decimal::divExact($one, $none);
} catch (ArithmeticError $exactByZero) {
    echo $exactByZero->message, "\n";
}
try {
    echo Core\Decimal::divRound($one, $none, 2, Core\RoundMode::HalfUp);
} catch (ArithmeticError $roundByZero) {
    echo $roundByZero->message, "\n";
}

// § 1 bounds a `decimal` at 28 fractional digits, so a wider scale is refused
// rather than quietly clamped to one that fits -- the same direction § 3 takes
// for a product whose scale would overflow.
try {
    echo Core\Decimal::divRound($one, 3, 40, Core\RoundMode::HalfUp);
} catch (ArithmeticError $tooWide) {
    echo $tooWide->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Decimal::divExact cannot divide by zero
Core\Decimal::divRound cannot divide by zero
Core\Decimal::divRound cannot answer 1 / 3 at scale 40: a `decimal` holds 28 fractional digits and a 96-bit mantissa
